{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1331 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "ffb9880863db7220b70885aa763bb5c2c5850a622f7a5961ab4f947a668761a9" :edb/db-before-t 1330 :edb/db-after-t 1331 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534643 50 #inst "2026-10-12T05:44:27.068Z" 13194139534643 17592186045417 true] [17592186118388 1075 #edb/ref 17592186118389 13194139534643 17592186045417 false] [17592186118388 1075 #edb/ref 17592186118390 13194139534643 17592186045417 false] [17592186118388 1075 #edb/ref 17592186118391 13194139534643 17592186045417 false] [17592186118388 1075 #edb/ref 17592186118392 13194139534643 17592186045417 false] [17592186118388 1075 #edb/ref 17592186118393 13194139534643 17592186045417 false] [17592186118388 1075 #edb/ref 17592186430711 13194139534643 17592186045417 true] [17592186118388 1075 #edb/ref 17592186430712 13194139534643 17592186045417 true] [17592186118388 1075 #edb/ref 17592186430714 13194139534643 17592186045417 true] [17592186118388 1075 #edb/ref 17592186430719 13194139534643 17592186045417 true] [17592186118388 1075 #edb/ref 17592186430723 13194139534643 17592186045417 true] [17592186118388 1078 #edb/ref 17592186430723 13194139534643 17592186045417 true] [17592186119632 1074 "We need to find $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix\n![](images/e5/e5b57b4f1488c927d64d89cb4352715b471025a111a39fe786c869973401fbd4.svg)\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -11 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -12 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix above corresponds to the following system of equations: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=-5 \\\\ -4{x}_{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2 =-1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_1 + 3 \\left( -1 \\right)  = -5\n\\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad\nx_1 = -2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/28/286b19d6c3b5b3fac7f65ffd4adab1b175d4dab5fab1e5bb7a85da692f09224a.svg)" 13194139534643 17592186045417 true] [17592186119632 1074 "We need to find $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix\n![](images/e5/e5b57b4f1488c927d64d89cb4352715b471025a111a39fe786c869973401fbd4.svg)\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -11 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -12 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix above corresponds to the following system of equations: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=-5 \\\\ -4{x}_{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2 =-1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_1 + 3 \\left( -1 \\right)  = -5\n\\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad\nx_1 = -2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/28/286b19d6c3b5b3fac7f65ffd4adab1b175d4dab5fab1e5bb7a85da692f09224a.svg)" 13194139534643 17592186045417 false] [17592186119633 1074 "We need to find $x_1,$ $x_2,$ and $x_3$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above system as an augmented matrix $M$ and reducing using Gaussian elimination, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ -9{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=7 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3}=\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $x_3 =2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-9x_2 - 2 = 7 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_2 = -1,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x_2=-1$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1+3(-1) = 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x_1 = 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/76/766d220a3c5bc2819ab05886751b6a1b782ece06c9d71b9baaaee19e9a2ecbdd.svg)" 13194139534643 17592186045417 true] [17592186119633 1074 "We need to find $x_1,$ $x_2,$ and $x_3$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above system as an augmented matrix $M$ and reducing using Gaussian elimination, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ -9{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=7 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3}=\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $x_3 =2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-9x_2 - 2 = 7 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_2 = -1,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x_2=-1$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1+3(-1) = 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x_1 = 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/76/766d220a3c5bc2819ab05886751b6a1b782ece06c9d71b9baaaee19e9a2ecbdd.svg)" 13194139534643 17592186045417 false] [17592186119820 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430732 13194139534643 17592186045417 true] [17592186119821 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430731 13194139534643 17592186045417 true] [17592186120358 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" 13194139534643 17592186045417 true] [17592186120358 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" 13194139534643 17592186045417 false] [17592186120358 1074 "We need to find ${x}_{1}$,  ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom this, we get the system with the augmented matrix $M$, which we reduce using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ -{x}_{3}=-1 \\\\ 0=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the fourth equation is always false. Therefore, the system has no solution and we conclude that \n\n$$\nx\\notin \\text{Span}\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}.\n$$" 13194139534643 17592186045417 true] [17592186120358 1074 "We need to find ${x}_{1}$,  ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom this, we get the system with the augmented matrix $M$, which we reduce using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ -{x}_{3}=-1 \\\\ 0=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the fourth equation is always false. Therefore, the system has no solution and we conclude that \n\n$$\nx\\notin \\text{Span}{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}.\n$$" 13194139534643 17592186045417 false] [17592186120379 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534643 17592186045417 true] [17592186120379 1071 "Find the vector $x$, if $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}}$ is a basis of $Span{b_{1}, b_{2}}$, and\n$b_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, b_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}, [x]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139534643 17592186045417 false] [17592186120379 1071 "Find the vector $x$, if $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\},$ and \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534643 17592186045417 true] [17592186120379 1074 "We know that any vector in  $\\text{Span}\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the coordinates relative to the basis $\\,B.$ Since we are told that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={{(\\,-3\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{{(\\,5\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 21 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534643 17592186045417 true] [17592186121042 1064 "Given a subspace $H$ with a basis $\\mathcal B = \\{ \\mathbf b_1, \\mathbf b_2 \\}$ and a vector $\\mathbf x \\in H,$ the **coordinates of the vector** $\\mathbf{x}$ **relative to the basis** $\\mathcal{B}$ are the coefficients ${\\color{red} x_1}$ and ${\\color{blue} x_2}$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}x_1} \\mathbf{b}_1+{\\color{blue}x_2} \\mathbf{b}_2 = \\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can write the vector $\\mathbf x$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal B$ using these coordinates, as follows:\n\n![](images/05/0593ca6a838b66c4623e8749eb5236d6259b1f9bf36ed0d68b83c49781076341.svg)\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\},$ where \n\n![](images/f3/f355faa0c477775f2a1cbb3522d0743cdbc9649a5ee9c55b579b1fac9ae2a661.svg)\n\nAlso let $H = \\textrm{span} \\{ \\mathbf b_1, \\mathbf b_2 \\},$ and consider the vector ![](images/78/78357fcf51c9cd6b7873f5bc35fcc214cb3790bcec00ee69d0932f15aacd39cb.svg)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf x$ can be expressed as a unique linear combination of $\\mathbf b_1$ and $\\mathbf b_2.$ That is to say, there exist *unique coefficients* $\\color{red}x_1$ and $\\color{blue}x_2$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's solve for these coefficients. From the above, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=8 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of this system is \n\n![](images/82/82d4f494936d23e9b697aa4518edf68f7ea62e504d9d2576a6d13971acaf1c96.svg)\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = {\\color{red}3} \\mathbf{b}_1+ {\\color{blue}2} \\mathbf{b}_2,\n$$\n\nwhich can be visualized as shown below.\n\n![](images/94/94b2934727a32284e9790e59ccd301bd858a0258843314a2968285a5eda7a302.png)" 13194139534643 17592186045417 true] [17592186121042 1064 "Given a subspace $H$ with a basis $\\mathcal B = \\{ \\mathbf b_1, \\mathbf b_2 \\}$ and a vector $\\mathbf x \\in H,$ the **coordinates of the vector** $\\mathbf{x}$ **relative to the basis** $\\mathcal{B}$ are the coefficients ${\\color{red} x_1}$ and ${\\color{blue} x_2}$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}x_1} \\mathbf{b}_1+{\\color{blue}x_2} \\mathbf{b}_2 = \\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can write the vector $\\mathbf x$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal B$ using these coordinates, as follows:\n\n![](images/05/0593ca6a838b66c4623e8749eb5236d6259b1f9bf36ed0d68b83c49781076341.svg)\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\},$ where \n\n![](images/f3/f355faa0c477775f2a1cbb3522d0743cdbc9649a5ee9c55b579b1fac9ae2a661.svg)\n\nAlso let $H = \\textrm{span} \\{ \\mathbf b_1, \\mathbf b_2 \\},$ and consider the vector ![](images/78/78357fcf51c9cd6b7873f5bc35fcc214cb3790bcec00ee69d0932f15aacd39cb.svg)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf x$ can be expressed as a unique linear combination of $\\mathbf b_1$ and $\\mathbf b_2.$ That is to say, there exist *unique coefficients* $\\color{red}x_1$ and $\\color{blue}x_2$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's solve for these coefficients. From the above, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=8 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of this system is \n\n![](images/82/82d4f494936d23e9b697aa4518edf68f7ea62e504d9d2576a6d13971acaf1c96.svg)\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = {\\color{red}3} \\mathbf{b}_1+ {\\color{blue}2} \\mathbf{b}_2,\n$$\n\nwhich can be visualized as shown below.\n\n![](images/94/94b2934727a32284e9790e59ccd301bd858a0258843314a2968285a5eda7a302.png)" 13194139534643 17592186045417 false] [17592186121066 1064 "Let's consider a set of vectors\n![](images/0d/0d78e1b2d01003885882f19b3856278e5024719f26f33594fd071fbd73273b71.svg)\n\nNotice that the set $\\mathcal{S}$ is linearly independent since the vector equation $x_1\\mathbf{e}_1+x_2\\mathbf{e}_2+x_3\\mathbf{e}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ leads to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich has only the zero solution. \n\nAlso, notice that any vector in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{e}_1,$ $\\mathbf{e}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{e}_3.$ For instance,\n![](images/ef/ef28dd8cbad6d256b9fa82c487263af3327ce630839cfcd1c7433417d0e0ae73.svg)\n\nIn particular, the equation above tells us that \n![](images/